  "texto": "Move five. The switch. One Friday, Maya said something small. A disagreement about weekend plans. Daniel didn't yell. He didn't even answer. He just went silent. For two days. He moved through the apartment like she wasn't there. She apologized. He didn't respond. She apologized again. Nothing. By Sunday night, she was begging to be forgiven for something she couldn't even name. Then, suddenly, warmth. A hug. 'I'm sorry I got so upset.' 'You know how much I love you.' And Maya felt something like relief. Almost like gratitude. Some days, he was the man from the first month again. Attentive. Funny. Perfect. And those days were enough to make her forget the others. Almost. That's the switch. Cruelty, then kindness, at unpredictable moments. It's the same mechanism that keeps people pulling the lever on a slot machine. Your brain doesn't bond to the kindness. It bonds to the relief after the pain. Researchers call it trauma bonding. And it's why leaving is so much harder than people think.",
  "voz": "en-US-AndrewNeural",
  "rate": "-4%",
  "capitulo": "C5",
  "nombre": "Move 5 - The switch (trauma bonding)"
}